INT. SALEM MEETINGHOUSE - EVENING

1692. Bare pine benches. A pulpit. A candle gutters beside a Bible.

GOODWIFE TABITHA CROMPTON, 50s, plain gray dress and white cap, kneels at the front pew with her eyes closed. A door creaks. She does not turn.

EZEKIEL PRUITT, 30s, a young magistrate in a black coat, steps inside and hangs his hat on a peg.

EZEKIEL
Goodwife Crompton.

TABITHA
Magistrate Pruitt.

EZEKIEL
They said you'd be here.

TABITHA
Where else would I be?

EZEKIEL
At home. With the door barred.

She opens her eyes. She looks at him, tired.

TABITHA
It's over, then.

EZEKIEL
The governor has pardoned the rest. Your name was the last he signed.

She bows her head. He crosses the floor and sits at the end of the bench, a respectful distance away. The candle gutters and steadies.
